WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.890
- You want a good quote about friendship?

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.270
I've got one for ya.

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.010
There's nothing better than a friend

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.730
unless it is a friend with chocolate.

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:09.100
It's so true.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.463
So, so good.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.233
- Speaking of friends,
(upbeat music)

00:00:12.233 --> 00:00:14.130
Donald Trump, he's not
all that big on them.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.810
Throughout his life in
politics and business,

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.150
Trump has surrounded himself

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:20.200
with a very small group of people.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.310
That group includes family,
fellow titans of industry

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.950
and some mixture of
hangers on and yes men,

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:29.130
and it's that last group, the
hangers on and the yes men,

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:30.400
that interest me most.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:31.340
Good job, boss.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:32.450
Because throughout his life,

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:36.500
Trump has proven that
there is some real value

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.690
in staying very close to him.

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:40.730
In his days as a real estate
developer and marketer

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:42.920
that value was primarily financial.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.620
Trump, the financial whale,
dropped off enough money

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.670
to enrich those minnows
swimming beside him,

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:48.940
who are willing to say and do

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.000
whatever he told them to say and do.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.580
Exhibit A: Michael Cohen.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:55.413
Right?

00:00:55.413 --> 00:00:56.246
Right.

00:00:56.246 --> 00:00:58.020
Now as president, the
value proposition for Trump

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.650
is influence and future political gain,

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:02.440
and just as in his life as a business man,

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:06.430
Trump has attracted a small,
but extremely loyal cadre

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:09.930
of House members and senators
who defend him to a fault,

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.300
understanding that being close to Trump

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.140
serves their larger goals.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.830
Okay, let's go through all
of these president's men,

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:20.500
and yes, they are pretty much

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:21.340
all men.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:24.300
And we start with Matt
Gaetz, a Florida congressman

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:27.390
who was once described
in a terrific headline

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:31.600
by GQ Magazine as the
Trumpiest congressman

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.470
in Trump's Washington.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:35.303
Well done.

00:01:35.303 --> 00:01:37.080
Way back in 2013, Gaetz was in a meeting

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.910
with one-time Trump political
Svengali Roger Stone,

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:41.780
yes, all roads lead back to Roger Stone,

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:44.700
when Stone told Gaetz that
the businessman Donald Trump

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:45.950
would be president one day.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.546
Quote, "I thought he had
gone one toke over the line,"

00:01:48.546 --> 00:01:50.470
said Gaetz of Stone's predictions.

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:51.710
Marijuana reference.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:54.030
But, Gaetz was convinced, somehow,

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:55.660
and became an early endorser of Trump,

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.750
and unlike so many other Republicans,

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.180
who wavered in their support

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.470
for Trump over the 2016 campaign,

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.370
Gaetz never, ever backed away

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:05.790
from the billionaire businessman.

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.260
- I think that the substance of this memo

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:11.100
absolutely implicates senior officials

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.090
of the Department of Justice and the FBI,

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.160
and I think hat it shows

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.470
that the entire Mueller investigation

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.422
should have never been
started in the first place.

00:02:18.422 --> 00:02:21.790
- In fact, the more norms
Trump has smashed through

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.640
as a candidate and now as president,

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.820
the more Gaetz has not only leaned in

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:27.095
to his support for the president

00:02:27.095 --> 00:02:31.060
but also seemed to emulate
Trump's actual behavior.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.770
On the eve of one-time
Trump fixer Michael Cohen...

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:34.603
Yeah, him.

00:02:34.603 --> 00:02:36.313
His testimony to Congress in mid-February,

00:02:36.313 --> 00:02:39.400
Gaetz sent out a tweet
that was clearly designed

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.490
to intimidate Cohen.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:42.323
Wrote Gaetz,

00:02:42.323 --> 00:02:43.167
(whoosh)
quote,

00:02:43.167 --> 00:02:45.220
"Hey @MichaelCohen212.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:48.460
Do your wife and father-in-law
know about your girlfriends?

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.010
Maybe tonight would be a
good time for that chat.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:55.411
I wonder if she'll remain
faithful when you're in prison.

00:02:55.411 --> 00:02:57.423
She's about to learn a lot."

00:02:57.423 --> 00:02:58.490
Dot, dot,

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:00.415
dot.
(whoosh)

00:03:00.415 --> 00:03:02.100
(sighs) That's a doozy.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:03.510
Gaetz later deleted that tweet,

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:05.140
but he did show up at Cohen's hearing

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.689
mostly to get attention,

00:03:06.689 --> 00:03:08.850
almost excursively to get attention,

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:11.550
but ostensibly to try to ask
questions despite the fact

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:14.851
that he's actually not on the
House Oversight Committee.

00:03:14.851 --> 00:03:16.660
(sighs heavily)

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:17.493
Moving on.

00:03:17.493 --> 00:03:19.190
There are two other House members

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:21.430
who are super close to Trump,

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:22.970
and they're two peas in a pod:

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:26.950
Ohio's Jim Jordan and North
Carolina's Mark Meadows.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:30.617
Now, the two actually
wrote a co-bylined op-ed

00:03:30.617 --> 00:03:31.670
(whoosh)
in USA Today

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:33.790
in advance of the Cohen hearing,

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:37.120
calling it, quote, a
disservice to the public,

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.180
an affront to our democratic values

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:43.730
and flat-out offensive to
anyone who seeks the truth,

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:44.563
end quote.
(whoosh)

00:03:44.563 --> 00:03:45.396
So who are they?

00:03:45.396 --> 00:03:46.670
All right, well Meadows
is the current chairman

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:48.010
of the House Freedom Caucus,

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:50.750
which is Trump's base in Congress
and the most conservative

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:53.810
and confrontational
Republicans in the House.

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:55.870
Jordan actually co-founded that group

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:59.370
and remains very closely
affiliated with it.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.780
Now Meadows and Jordan have
worked hammer and tongs...

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:03.700
I love that expression...

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:06.400
Since Trump was elected
to help him build his case

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.660
that there's in fact a deep state

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:12.030
within the Justice Department
and the broader government

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:15.480
that never wanted Trump to
win and, since he did win,

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.950
has worked to hamstring his effectiveness.

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:21.620
Now both men, Meadows and Jordan,

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:22.770
not only make their case for Trump

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:24.550
inside the walls of Congress.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:27.640
They also do it on cable TV,
including Trump's pet channel,

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.010
which, of course, I
don't need tell you this,

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:29.980
is Fox News channel.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:31.390
- The American people have a right to know

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:33.170
if the guy running the Justice Department

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:35.870
was actually talking about
doing those two things

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:37.420
with the guy they elected president.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:38.253
They need to write...

00:04:38.253 --> 00:04:39.600
They need answers to those questions.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:40.850
- Trump has, of course, taken notice

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:43.280
'cause all he does is watch cable TV.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:44.270
Quote, he's a champ.

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:45.557
I love him defending me on television.

00:04:45.557 --> 00:04:46.915
"He is a bulldog," Trump said of Jordan

00:04:46.915 --> 00:04:48.750
at a campaign rally in Cleveland,

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:49.830
just before the election.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:51.410
Quote, he doesn't give a damn.

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:52.420
Boom, boom, boom!

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:53.520
Point after point!

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:54.353
Right?!

00:04:54.353 --> 00:04:55.530
He's a champ.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:56.605
Boom, boom, boom!
(banging)

00:04:56.605 --> 00:04:58.210
Meadows bragged in the Cohen hearing

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:01.630
that he had spoken to
Trump more than 300 times.

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:02.630
I bet you haven't done that.

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:03.910
Right, right, am I right?

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:04.743
I'm right.

00:05:04.743 --> 00:05:06.630
Meadows was also considered
a potential chief of staff,

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:07.463
Trump in the White House,

00:05:07.463 --> 00:05:08.860
but he didn't get that job.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.010
Now let's move over to the Senate

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:13.160
and the very curious
case of Lindsey Graham.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:15.720
Graham ran against Trump
in the 2016 GOP primaries,

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:17.140
and although the South Carolina senator

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:19.010
was never a serious contender,

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:23.520
he was outspoken about his
distaste for Donald John Trump.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.240
Said Graham of Trump on
CNN in December 2015,

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:29.640
quote, "He's a race-baiting,
xenophobic religious bigot.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.100
He doesn't represent my party.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:33.620
He doesn't represent the
values that the men and women

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:36.650
who wear the uniform are
fighting for," end quote.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:38.880
Seems pretty cut and dry,
but Graham has experienced

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:41.440
a remarkable about face since those days.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:43.810
He is now a regular golf
partner of the President's

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:47.450
and one of Trump's most
outspoken defenders.

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:48.283
Take this.

00:05:48.283 --> 00:05:50.720
Graham almost single
handedly saved the nomination

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:52.670
of Brett Kavanaugh to the Supreme Court,

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:55.710
dragging a badly damaged
Kavanaugh across the line

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:58.370
with a spirited defense
of the judge's record

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:02.310
and character in front of the
Senate Judiciary Committee.

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:04.590
And, yes, Graham defended Trump

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.760
about the Cohen hearing
as well, tweeting this,

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:07.593
quote,
(whoosh)

00:06:07.593 --> 00:06:09.350
Democrats' hatred of Trump is undercutting

00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:12.470
an important foreign
policy effort and is way

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:13.303
out of line.
(whoosh)

00:06:13.303 --> 00:06:14.680
That important foreign policy effort?

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:17.960
The summit between Kim Jong Un
and Donald Trump in Vietnam.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.320
So, what explains Graham's 180 on Trump?

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:24.060
Graham himself explained it
as a desire to be relevant,

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.180
in a recent New York
Times Magazine profile,

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:28.480
which he probably shouldn't have said.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:31.030
The desire to be relevant
is a powerful elixir

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:33.240
in politics and life,
and that's what explains

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.920
the closeness of all
these president's men.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:38.530
They think that by staying
in Trump's good graces,

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:41.100
they have a better chance
of getting what they want

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.910
in policy and political terms.

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:45.180
And that

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:46.400
is the point.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:48.760
We make new episodes every
Tuesday and Thursday.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:49.848
Make sure to check them all out.

00:06:49.848 --> 00:06:52.431
(upbeat music)

